import json
from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2];data=json.loads((R/'data/americas.json').read_text());c=json.loads((R/'research/americas-coverage.json').read_text());new=[];rr={};nn={};held={}
def a(y,n,d,t,s,st,sol,why):
 rr.setdefault(y,{})[str(n)]=d;nn.setdefault(y,{})[str(n)]=why
 if not 4<=d<=7:return
 new.append(dict(id=f'ca-cmo-{y}-{n}',country='Canada',competition='CMO',year=y,section='National final',number=str(n),statement=st,solution=sol,sourceUrl=f'https://cms.math.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/exam{y}.pdf',solutionKind='original-exposition',topic=t,tags=s,subtopics=s,difficulty=d,ratingReason=why,reviewStatus='individually-assessed',notes='Statement restated in original wording. Official CMS paper checked; original independent proof supplied because no worked solution is linked in the official archive.'))
def low(y,n,d,why):a(y,n,d,'Algebra',[],None,None,why)
def hold(y,n,why):held.setdefault(y,{})[str(n)]=why
low(1981,1,3.5,'If x<196, the sum of the six floors is at most195+391+783+1567+3135+6271=12342. If x≥196, it is at least63·196=12348. The target lies in the skipped interval.')
a(1981,2,4,'Geometry',['Geometric inequalities','Coordinates & vectors'],r'A circle of radius $r$ is tangent at $P$ to a line. For $R$ on the circle, let $Q$ be its perpendicular projection onto that tangent. Find the largest area of triangle $PQR$.',r'Use $P=(0,0)$ and centre $(0,r)$. For $R=(x,y)$, we have $x^2=2ry-y^2$ and the squared area is $y^3(2r-y)/4$. Apply AM–GM to $y/3,y/3,y/3,2r-y$, whose sum is $2r$, to get $y^3(2r-y)\le27r^4/16$. Equality occurs at $y=3r/2$. The maximum area is therefore $3\sqrt3r^2/8$.','The area is a one-variable product with unequal exponents; splitting the repeated factor into three parts gives a sharp AM–GM bound.')
low(1981,3,3.5,'For finitely many lines, move a centre far along a direction parallel to none; all distances grow without bound. For infinitely many lines, the vertical lines with rational x-coordinate meet every positive-radius circle.')
a(1981,4,4,'Algebra',['Polynomials','Functions & graphs','Functional equations'],r'Real polynomials $P,Q$ satisfy $P(Q(x))=Q(P(x))$ for all real $x$. If $P(x)=Q(x)$ has no real solution, prove that $P(P(x))=Q(Q(x))$ also has no real solution.',r'The continuous function $D=P-Q$ has one strict sign on the real line. Suppose $P(P(x))=Q(Q(x))$, and put $a=P(x)$, $b=Q(x)$. The supposed equality gives $P(a)=Q(b)$, while commutation gives $P(b)=Q(a)$. Hence $D(a)+D(b)=0$, impossible when both values have the same strict sign.','Commutation relates the two crossed evaluations; combining them with the proposed equality creates opposite signs and invokes continuity.')
a(1981,5,6,'Combinatorics',['Sets & partitions','Counting','Extremal arguments'],r'Eleven theatre groups attend a festival. On each day some groups perform and the others watch. Every group must watch at least one performance of every other group. What is the minimum number of days?',r'The answer is six. Assign to each group its set of performing days. For two groups to watch one another, neither set may contain the other; thus the eleven sets form an antichain. On five days, count permutations of the days in which a particular set of size $k$ is an initial segment: there are $k!(5-k)!$. At most one set in an antichain can be an initial segment of the same permutation, so $\sum_S1/\binom5{|S|}\le1$. Since every binomial coefficient here is at most10, there can be at most10 groups. Fewer days cannot help, by adding unused days. For six days, choose eleven distinct three-element subsets as performing schedules; any two are incomparable.','The schedules form an antichain rather than just distinct binary codes; a permutation-counting proof of the five-day upper bound is needed for the exact minimum.')
a(1982,1,4,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Area & dissection'],r'For a convex quadrilateral $A_1A_2A_3A_4$, define points $B_i$ by $\overrightarrow{OB_i}=\overrightarrow{A_iA_{i+1}}$, with $A_5=A_1$. Prove $[B_1B_2B_3B_4]=2[A_1A_2A_3A_4]$.',r'Write $a_i$ for the position vectors of $A_i$ and $b_i=a_{i+1}-a_i$. The signed doubled area of the B-quadrilateral is $\sum_i\det(b_i,b_{i+1})$. Expand every determinant. The adjacent-vertex terms occur twice, while the opposite-vertex terms cancel in pairs, giving $\sum_i\det(b_i,b_{i+1})=2\sum_i\det(a_i,a_{i+1})$. Taking absolute values proves the area relation.','The construction becomes successive edge vectors; expanding the shoelace determinants exposes a cancellation that gives the exact factor of two.')
a(1982,2,4.5,'Algebra',['Polynomials','Sequences & recurrences'],r'Let $a,b,c$ be the roots of $x^3-x^2-x-1$. Prove that they are distinct and that $\frac{a^{1982}-b^{1982}}{a-b}+\frac{b^{1982}-c^{1982}}{b-c}+\frac{c^{1982}-a^{1982}}{c-a}$ is an integer.',r'The derivative is $(3x+1)(x-1)$, and neither $1$ nor $-1/3$ is a root of the cubic, so the roots are distinct. For $m\ge0$, the coefficient of $t^m$ in $1/((1-at)(1-bt))$ is $(a^{m+1}-b^{m+1})/(a-b)$. Summing over the three pairs gives the formal series $(3-(a+b+c)t)/((1-at)(1-bt)(1-ct))=(3-t)/(1-t-t^2-t^3)$, by Vieta. Every coefficient of this series is integral, since the denominator has constant term1 and integer coefficients. Take the coefficient of $t^{1981}$.','The large exponent is handled symbolically: a generating function packages the divided differences and Vieta makes the entire series integral.')
a(1982,3,5.5,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Solid geometry'],r'For $n\ge1$, determine the smallest size of a finite set $S\subset\mathbb R^n$ such that every point of $\mathbb R^n$ is at irrational distance from at least one point of $S$.',r'For $n=1$, the answer is2: use $0$ and $\sqrt2$. A point at rational distance from both would make their difference rational. One point never suffices. For $n\ge2$, two points never suffice: on their perpendicular bisector one can choose a point at any sufficiently large integer distance from both. Three do suffice. Put them at $0,e,te$ on a line, where $e$ is a unit vector and $t=\sqrt[3]2$. If a point $z$ had rational distances $r_0,r_1,r_t$ to all three, subtracting squared distances gives $q=z\cdot e=(r_0^2+1-r_1^2)/2\in\mathbb Q$. Then $t^2-2qt+(r_0^2-r_t^2)=0$, impossible since $\sqrt[3]2$ has degree3 over $\mathbb Q$. Thus the answer is3 for all $n\ge2$.','The lower bound is geometric, while a three-point collinear construction uses an algebraic number of degree three to obstruct rational squared distances.')
a(1982,4,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Permutations & arrangements','Recurrences & generating functions'],r'Let $f_n$ count permutations of $n$ objects with no fixed point, and $g_n$ count those with exactly one fixed point. Prove $|f_n-g_n|=1$.',r'Let $f_0=1$. Choosing the unique fixed point gives $g_n=nf_{n-1}$. The standard derangement decomposition gives $f_n=(n-1)(f_{n-1}+f_{n-2})$: choose the image $j$ of1; either $j$ maps back to1, leaving a derangement of $n-2$ objects, or remove1 from its cycle, leaving a derangement of $n-1$ objects. Hence $f_n-nf_{n-1}=-[f_{n-1}-(n-1)f_{n-2}]$. Starting with $f_1-f_0=-1$, the difference alternates between $-1$ and1.','A near-cancellation between two permutation classes becomes exact only after deriving and rearranging the derangement recurrence.')
a(1982,5,4.5,'Geometry',['Solid geometry','Coordinates & vectors'],r'In a tetrahedron of volume $V$, let $h_A,h_B,h_C,h_D$ be the altitudes. Move each vertex outward along its altitude by $k/h_A,k/h_B,k/h_C,k/h_D$, respectively, for fixed $k>0$. Prove that the centroid is unchanged.',r'Let $F_A$ be the area of the face opposite $A$, and $n_A$ its outward unit normal. Since $F_Ah_A=3V$, the displacement of $A$ is $kF_A n_A/(3V)$. The four outward area vectors of a tetrahedron sum to zero. To see this, express each oriented face area vector as one half the sum of the cross products of its successive vertex vectors; every edge occurs twice with opposite orientations and cancels. Thus the sum of the four vertex displacements is zero, so their average, the centroid, is unchanged.','Reciprocal altitudes become face areas after multiplying by the common volume; the closed-surface area-vector identity supplies the cancellation.')
low(1983,1,2.5,'Every summand factorial is smaller than w!, so w!≤3(w−1)!, giving w≤3. Positivity forces w=3, and then x=y=z=2.')
a(1983,2,4.5,'Algebra',['Functional equations','Functions & graphs'],r'For each real $r$, transform the plane by $(x,y)\mapsto(2^r x,r2^r x+2^r y)$. Find all real functions $f$ whose graphs are unchanged by every such transformation.',r'Invariance is equivalent to $f(2^r x)=2^r f(x)+r2^r x$. Taking $x=0$ gives $f(0)=0$. For $x>0$, take the starting point1 and $r=\log_2x$, obtaining $f(x)=x(\log_2x+C_+)$ where $C_+=f(1)$. For $x<0$, start at $-1$ and obtain $f(x)=x(\log_2|x|+C_-)$ where $C_-=-f(-1)$. Conversely, these formulas satisfy the transformation identity for arbitrary constants $C_+,C_-$, and inverse transformations are also present, so the graphs are unchanged.','Scaling has separate positive and negative orbits; dividing by x reveals a logarithmic translation law and leaves two independent constants.')
a(1983,3,5,'Geometry',['Solid geometry','Coordinates & vectors'],r'Are the four face areas of a tetrahedron sufficient to determine its volume? Prove your answer.',r'No. Consider the four vertices $(a,b,c),(a,-b,-c),(-a,b,-c),(-a,-b,c)$ for $a,b,c>0$. All four face areas equal $2\sqrt{a^2b^2+b^2c^2+c^2a^2}$, by a cross-product calculation, while the volume is $8abc/3$. Taking $(a,b,c)=(1,1,1)$ gives face areas $2\sqrt3$ and volume $8/3$. Taking $(a,b,c)=(\sqrt{3/2},\sqrt{3/2},1/2)$ gives the same four face areas but volume2.','A counterexample needs simultaneous control of all four faces. The symmetric coordinate family makes them congruent while leaving the volume adjustable.')
a(1983,4,4.5,'Number Theory',['Modular arithmetic','Primes'],r'For every prime $p$, prove that infinitely many positive integers $n$ satisfy $2^n\equiv n\pmod p$.',r'For $p=2$, every even positive $n$ works. For odd $p$, choose $n\equiv1\pmod{p-1}$ and $n\equiv2\pmod p$. The Chinese remainder theorem gives one residue class modulo $p(p-1)$, hence infinitely many positive choices. Fermat gives $2^n\equiv2\pmod p$, equal to $n$ as required.','The exponent and the value of n are controlled in two coprime moduli; the Chinese remainder theorem joins the two requirements.')
low(1983,5,3.5,'Take logarithms. Across all nonempty subsets, each original logarithm has the same total coefficient, and the sum of all coefficients is 2^n−1; dividing by that count gives coefficient1/n for every original number.')
a(1984,1,4.5,'Number Theory',['Modular arithmetic','Valuations','Squares & powers'],r'Prove that the sum of the squares of any $1984$ consecutive positive integers is not a square.',r'Since $1984=31\cdot64$, each residue modulo64 occurs31 times. Therefore the sum is congruent modulo64 to $31\sum_{j=0}^{63}j^2=31\cdot32\cdot21\cdot127\equiv32\pmod{64}$. It is divisible by $2^5$ but not $2^6$, so its2-adic valuation is odd. A square has even valuation at every prime, proving the claim.','The relevant modulus is64, matching the exact power of two in the interval length; the residue sum forces an odd valuation.')
a(1984,2,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Colouring','Permutations & arrangements','Symmetry'] if False else ['Colouring','Permutations & arrangements'],r'Keys occupy $n$ places on a circular ring, which may be rotated or turned over. Each key is given a colour. What is the fewest colours needed so that every key can be identified from the entire cyclic colour pattern?',r'The answer is1 for $n=1$,2 for $n=2$,3 for $n=3,4,5$, and2 for $n\ge6$. Identification means that no nonidentity rotation or reflection preserves the colouring. For $n=3,4,5$, in any two-colouring the less frequent colour appears at most twice. If it appears once, reflection through that key preserves the pattern; if twice, reflection exchanging the two positions preserves it; the monochromatic case also fails. Three colours suffice: give two adjacent positions distinct unique colours and all others the third colour. For $n\ge6$, use two colours, with exactly three marked positions whose consecutive cyclic gaps are $1,2,n-3$. These three gaps are distinct, so neither a nontrivial rotation nor a reflection preserves the set of marked positions. One colour clearly fails when $n>1$.','The small cycles behave differently from the general case. An asymmetric three-mark pattern gives a uniform two-colour construction, while reflections obstruct it for n≤5.')
a(1984,3,4,'Number Theory',['Digits & bases','Divisibility & GCD','Induction'],r'Call a positive integer digitally divisible if it has no zero digits and is divisible by the sum of its digits. Prove that infinitely many such integers exist.',r'Use the repunit $R_{3^k}$ containing $3^k$ copies of1. Its digit sum is $3^k$. Starting with $R_1=1$, the identity $R_{3m}=R_m(10^{2m}+10^m+1)$ shows that each tripling of the length adds a factor divisible by3. Induction gives $3^k\mid R_{3^k}$. These lengths are unbounded, so the construction supplies infinitely many examples.','The number of digits must divide the repunit itself; tripling the length creates exactly the divisibility needed for an infinite family.')
a(1984,4,4.5,'Geometry',['Triangle centres','Geometric inequalities','Trigonometry'],r'An acute triangle has area $1$. Prove that some point inside it is at distance at least $2/\sqrt[4]{27}$ from every vertex.',r'Take the circumcentre, which lies inside an acute triangle. If its radius is $R$ and the angles are $A,B,C$, then $1=2R^2\sin A\sin B\sin C$. The function $\log\sin t$ is concave on $(0,\pi)$, since its second derivative is $-1/\sin^2t$; hence the product of the three sines is at most $(\sin(\pi/3))^3=3\sqrt3/8$. Thus $1\le3\sqrt3R^2/4$, giving $R\ge2/\sqrt[4]{27}$. Equality occurs for an equilateral triangle.','The acute hypothesis identifies the circumcentre as an admissible point; maximizing the area of a triangle on a fixed circle gives the sharp radius bound.')
a(1984,5,4,'Algebra',['Trigonometry','Pigeonhole principle'],r'Given seven real numbers, prove that two can be labelled $x,y$ so that $0\le(x-y)/(1+xy)\le1/\sqrt3$.',r'Write the numbers as tangents of angles in $(-\pi/2,\pi/2)$. Split that interval into six intervals of length $\pi/6$. Two of the seven angles lie in one interval; label them $\alpha\ge\beta$. Their difference lies in $[0,\pi/6]$, so $0\le\tan(\alpha-\beta)\le1/\sqrt3$. The tangent subtraction formula gives the desired ratio. Its denominator is positive because $1+xy=\cos(\alpha-\beta)/(\cos\alpha\cos\beta)>0$.','The fractional expression suggests tangent subtraction; pigeonholing the transformed angles also ensures the denominator is nonzero with the required sign.')
a(1985,1,4.5,'Geometry',['Area & dissection','Triangle geometry'],r'Prove that exactly one line simultaneously bisects the area and the perimeter of a triangle with sides $6,8,10$.',r'The area is24 and semiperimeter12. A cutting line joins points on two sides meeting at some vertex with angle $\theta$; if their distances from that vertex are $u,v$, perimeter bisection gives $u+v=12$, while area bisection gives $uv\sin\theta=24$. At the right-angle vertex, $uv=24$, so $u,v=6\pm2\sqrt3$; the larger exceeds both available legs, so this is impossible. At the vertex between sides6 and10, $\sin\theta=4/5$, hence $uv=30$ and $u,v=6\pm\sqrt6$. Exactly one assignment fits: the smaller on side6 and the larger on side10. At the vertex between sides8 and10, $\sin\theta=3/5$, requiring $uv=40$, impossible with sum12. Thus the middle case gives exactly one line.','Both bisections translate to sum and product conditions, but checking all three possible corner triangles and their side-length feasibility is essential for uniqueness.')
low(1985,2,3.5,'If the first digit is d and the remaining k-digit block is N, the condition is8N=d(2·10^k−1). Thus8 divides d, forcing d=8 and N=2·10^k−1, too large for the remaining block.')
a(1985,3,4,'Geometry',['Geometric inequalities','Trigonometry'],r'A regular $1985$-gon is circumscribed around a circle of circumference $c$, and another regular $1985$-gon is inscribed in it. If their perimeters are $x,y$, prove $x+y\ge2c$.',r'Put $N=1985$ and $\theta=\pi/N$. For circle radius $r$, the perimeters are $x=2Nr\tan\theta$, $y=2Nr\sin\theta$. Since $(1+\cos\theta)^2\ge4\cos\theta$, we have $\tan\theta+\sin\theta\ge4\tan(\theta/2)\ge2\theta$. Therefore $x+y\ge4Nr\theta=4\pi r=2c$.','The separate tangent and sine bounds go in opposite directions; a half-angle comparison combines them into a useful lower bound.')
a(1985,4,4,'Number Theory',['Valuations','Factorials & binomial coefficients','Digits & bases'],r'Prove that $2^{n-1}$ divides $n!$ exactly when $n$ is a power of two.',r'Write the binary expansion $n=\sum_j\epsilon_j2^j$, and let $s_2(n)=\sum_j\epsilon_j$. Counting multiples of powers of2 in the factorial gives $v_2(n!)=\sum_{i\ge1}\lfloor n/2^i\rfloor=\sum_j\epsilon_j(2^j-1)=n-s_2(n)$. Thus $v_2(n!)\ge n-1$ exactly when $s_2(n)\le1$. Since $n>0$, this means exactly one binary digit is1.','Legendre’s count must be converted to binary digit sum; the near-maximal valuation then characterizes powers of two.')
a(1985,5,4.5,'Algebra',['Sequences & recurrences','Inequalities'],r'Let $1<x_1<2$ and $x_{n+1}=1+x_n-x_n^2/2$. Prove $|x_n-\sqrt2|<2^{-n}$ for every $n\ge3$.',r'The map $F(x)=1+x-x^2/2$ decreases on $[1,2]$ and sends this interval into $[1,3/2]$, which is invariant. Thus $x_3\in[11/8,3/2]$, giving $|x_3-\sqrt2|<1/8$. Since $F(\sqrt2)=\sqrt2$, we have $|x_{n+1}-\sqrt2|=|x_n-\sqrt2|\,|(x_n+\sqrt2)/2-1|$. On $[1,3/2]$, the final factor is positive and at most $(2\sqrt2-1)/4<1/2$. Induction proves the stated estimate.','An initial trapping interval gives a uniform contraction factor; checking the third term separately is needed for the exact power-of-two bound.')
a(1986,1,4.5,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Triangle geometry'],r'Point $C$ lies inside segment $AD$. Suppose $AB=CD=1$, $\angle ABC=90^\circ$, and $\angle CBD=30^\circ$, with $BC$ inside $\angle ABD$. Find $AC$.',r'Write $AC=u$ and place $B=(0,0)$, $A=(0,1)$, $C=(t,0)$ with $t>0$. Then $u^2=t^2+1$. Since $CD=1$ on the extension of $AC$, we have $D=(t(1+1/u),-1/u)$. The $30^\circ$ angle gives $t(u+1)=\sqrt3$. Consequently $(u^2-1)(u+1)^2=3$, or $u^4+2u^3-2u-4=(u+2)(u^3-2)=0$. Since $u>0$, $AC=\sqrt[3]2$.','Coordinates turn the linked length and angle conditions into a quartic whose unexpected factorization gives a cube root.')
a(1986,2,5,'Combinatorics',['Logic & information','Counting'],r'A,B,C contest $M$ athletic events. In every event the integer awards satisfy $p_1>p_2>p_3>0$. Their total scores are $22,9,9$, respectively. B wins the 100 metres. Find $M$ and the runner-up in the high jump.',r'The total gives $M(p_1+p_2+p_3)=40$, so $M\le6$ and $M\in\{1,2,4,5\}$. Since B won an event, $p_1+(M-1)p_3\le9$, so $p_1\le10-M$; also A loses at least one event, giving $22\le Mp_1-1$. This excludes $M=1,2$. If $M=4$, the award triples summing to10 are $(7,2,1),(6,3,1),(5,4,1),(5,3,2)$. The first makes B score at least10; the second lets A score at most $3\cdot6+3=21$; the last two let A score at most20. Hence $M=5$. The award triples summing to8 are $(5,2,1),(4,3,1)$; only the first can give A22. A must win four events and finish second in the 100 metres. B then finishes third in the other four, and C finishes second in them. Thus C is runner-up in the high jump.','The score totals first restrict the number of events and award triples; the known winner then forces all placements, including an event not named in the totals.')
a(1986,3,4,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Circles & tangency'],r'A chord $ST$ of fixed length has both endpoints on a fixed semicircle. Let $M$ be its midpoint and $P$ the projection of $S$ onto the diameter line. Prove that $\angle SPM$ is constant whenever it is defined.',r'Normalize the semicircle to the upper unit circle. Write $S=(\cos(\theta+\alpha),\sin(\theta+\alpha))$ and $T=(\cos(\theta-\alpha),\sin(\theta-\alpha))$, where the fixed half-angle $\alpha$ determines the chord length. Then $M=(\cos\alpha\cos\theta,\cos\alpha\sin\theta)$ and $P=(\cos(\theta+\alpha),0)$. Thus $\overrightarrow{PM}=\sin\theta(\sin\alpha,\cos\alpha)$, whose angle with the vertical $PS$ is $\alpha$, independent of $\theta$. Interchanging the endpoint labels changes the horizontal sign but not this angle.','The midpoint coordinates factor into a variable scalar times a fixed direction, which makes the moving angle constant.')
low(1986,4,3.5,'Pair r with n−r modulo n, and r with n+1−r modulo n+1. For odd powers both show2F(n,k) divisible by the respective modulus. Coprimality gives n(n+1)|2F(n,k).')
a(1986,5,4.5,'Number Theory',['Sequences & recurrences','Modular arithmetic'],r'Let $u_1=39$, $u_2=45$, and $u_{n+2}=u_{n+1}^2-u_n$. Prove that infinitely many terms are divisible by $1986$.',r'The third term is $45^2-39=1986$. Modulo1986, the state transition on ordered pairs is $(a,b)\mapsto(b,b^2-a)$, with inverse $(c,d)\mapsto(c^2-d,c)$. It is therefore a permutation of a finite set. Every state lies on a cycle, so the initial pair recurs periodically, as does its following zero residue. Thus infinitely many $u_n$ are divisible by1986.','An early zero alone does not imply infinitely many. Reversibility upgrades eventual periodicity of a finite-state recurrence to periodicity from the start.')
a(1987,1,4.5,'Number Theory',['Diophantine equations','Squares & powers','Modular arithmetic'],r'Find all positive integers $a\le b$ and $n<14$ satisfying $a^2+b^2=n!$.',r'The answers are $(a,b,n)=(1,1,2)$ and $(12,24,6)$. If a prime $p\equiv3\pmod4$ divides a sum of two squares, it divides both bases, so its valuation in that sum is even. The prime3 has odd valuation in $n!$ for $n=3,4,5$, and7 has valuation1 for $7\le n\le13$, excluding those cases. The case $n=1$ is impossible and $n=2$ is immediate. For $n=6$, reduction modulo3 makes both $a,b$ multiples of3; after division the sum is80, and modulo4 makes the new bases even. Thus $a=6u,b=6v$ with $u^2+v^2=20$. Checking $u=1,2,3$ gives only $(u,v)=(2,4)$.','Small factorials are best eliminated by the parity of valuations at primes3 modulo4; the surviving case still requires a short divisibility descent.')
a(1987,2,4,'Number Theory',['Digits & bases','Divisibility & GCD'],r'The integer $1987$ has a three-digit representation $xyz$ in base $b$, and the three digits sum to $25$. Find all possible digits and bases.',r'Reducing the numeral modulo $b-1$ gives $b-1\mid1987-25=1962=2\cdot3^2\cdot109$. Since the digit sum is25, $b\ge10$; since the numeral has three digits, $b^2\le1987$, so $b\le44$. The only divisors of1962 between9 and43 are9 and18, giving $b=10$ or19. Base10 cannot represent1987 in three digits. In base19, division gives $1987=5\cdot19^2+9\cdot19+11$, with digit sum25. Thus $(x,y,z,b)=(5,9,11,19)$.','The digit sum creates a strong divisor condition on one less than the base; length bounds reduce that condition to two cases.')
a(1987,3,6,'Geometry',['Triangle geometry','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals'],r'Let $ABCD$ be a parallelogram and let $E$ lie inside $BC$. If triangles $BAD,BED,DEC$ are all isosceles, determine every possible value of $\angle DAB$.',r'Measure angles in degrees. Put $\theta=\angle DAB$, $\alpha=\angle CBD$, and $\beta=\angle BDE$. The angle triples in the three triangles are respectively $(\theta,\alpha,180-\theta-\alpha)$, $(\alpha,\beta,180-\alpha-\beta)$, and $(\theta,\alpha+\beta,180-\theta-\alpha-\beta)$. Thus choose one equation from each row: (I) $2\theta+\alpha=180$, $\theta=\alpha$, or $\theta+2\alpha=180$; (II) $\alpha=\beta$, $2\alpha+\beta=180$, or $\alpha+2\beta=180$; (III) $2\theta+\alpha+\beta=180$, $\theta+2\alpha+2\beta=180$, or $\theta=\alpha+\beta$. Solving these27 linear systems and retaining positive $\theta,\alpha,\beta$ with sum less than180 gives exactly $(\theta,\alpha,\beta)=(540/7,180/7,180/7),(72,36,36),(45,45,45),(36,36,36),(36,36,72),(36,72,36),(180/7,540/7,360/7)$. Every retained triple constructs a triangle $BCD$ and an internal ray $DE$, so every case occurs. The distinct answers are $180/7,36,45,72,540/7$ degrees.','Three isosceles conditions each have three possible equal-angle choices. An exhaustive angle-system analysis, including positivity and an existence check, is required to avoid losing the nonstandard seventh-turn cases.')
a(1987,4,4,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Extremal arguments'],r'An odd number of people stand in a field. Each person has a unique nearest neighbour and fires a water pistol at that person. Prove that someone is not hit. Does this conclusion always hold for an even number?',r'Draw an arrow from each person to their nearest neighbour. A directed cycle of length at least3 is impossible: at each vertex the outgoing distance is strictly smaller than the incoming distance, giving a strict chain around a cycle. Thus every directed cycle has length2. If everyone were hit, each vertex would have indegree at least1; since the total indegree equals the number of vertices, every indegree would be1. The graph would then be a disjoint union of directed cycles, all of length2, forcing an even total. For an even number, place people in well-separated pairs, with the distance within each pair much smaller than any distance between pairs; everyone is hit.','The nearest-neighbour rule forces directed cycles to be mutual pairs; a global indegree count then converts oddness into an unhit person.')
low(1987,5,3.5,'The square of √n+√(n+1) lies strictly between4n+1 and4n+2. No square lies between4n+1 and4n+3, since squares are0 or1 modulo4, so all stated floors agree.')
low(1988,1,2,'Subtracting the equations forces x²=1; substitution gives b=±10879.')
low(1988,2,3,'The enlarged region is the triangle, three rectangular strips of total area5P, and circular sectors whose total angle is2π. Total area is A+5P+25π.')
a(1988,3,4,'Geometry',['Combinatorial geometry','Area & dissection','Extremal arguments'],r'At least five points in the plane are coloured red or blue, with no three points of one colour collinear. Prove that there is a monochromatic triangle having a side that contains no point of the other colour.',r'There is a monochromatic triangle by the pigeonhole principle. Choose one of least positive area among all such triangles. If each side contained a point of the opposite colour, choose one on each side. These three points form a nondegenerate monochromatic triangle, by the no-collinearity assumption, and it lies strictly inside the original in area because all three chosen points are interior to its sides. This contradicts minimality.','Selecting a smallest monochromatic area turns three blocked sides into a strictly smaller triangle of the opposite colour.')
a(1988,4,4,'Algebra',['Sequences & recurrences','Algebraic identities'],r'Let $x_0=0,x_1=1$, $y_0=1,y_1=2$, and let both sequences satisfy $z_{n+1}=4z_n-z_{n-1}$. Prove $y_n^2=3x_n^2+1$ for every $n\ge0$.',r'The numbers $2\pm\sqrt3$ satisfy $t^2=4t-1$. Hence the sequence $(2+\sqrt3)^n$ satisfies the same recurrence as $y_n+x_n\sqrt3$, and their first two values agree. Thus $y_n+x_n\sqrt3=(2+\sqrt3)^n$. Conjugating gives $y_n-x_n\sqrt3=(2-\sqrt3)^n$. Multiplication yields $y_n^2-3x_n^2=1$.','The coupled quadratic identity becomes a multiplicative norm after combining the two sequences into one quadratic-field expression.')
a(1988,5,4.5,'Number Theory',['Divisibility & GCD','Diophantine equations'],r'For a finite sequence $S=(a_1,\ldots,a_r)$ of integers, average the products over its $2^r-1$ nonempty selections of positions. Suppose this average is $13$, and appending a positive integer $a_{r+1}$ changes it to $49$. Determine all possibilities.',r'The sum of all nonempty products is $\prod_i(1+a_i)-1$. Thus $\prod_{i=1}^r(1+a_i)=13\cdot2^r-12$, and $(a_{r+1}+1)(13\cdot2^r-12)=98\cdot2^r-48$. For $r=1,2$, the ratio on the right divided by the old product is respectively $74/7,43/5$, not integral. For $r=3$ it is8, while for $r\ge4$ it lies strictly between7 and8. Hence $r=3$ and $a_4=7$. The three nonzero integers $1+a_1,1+a_2,1+a_3$ must have product92. Up to order, their absolute values are $(1,1,92),(1,2,46),(1,4,23),(2,2,23)$, and their signs have an even number of negatives. Subtract1 from each signed factor to obtain every solution. If one additionally requires all original $a_i$ positive, only the permutation $(1,1,22)$ remains, but the printed paper permits arbitrary integers.','The subset-product expansion fixes the sequence length before a signed factorization. The original integer wording allows zero and negative entries, which must not be silently excluded.')
low(1989,1,3,'Read the order backwards: the final entry must be the least or greatest remaining number. Each of the first n−1 removals has two choices, giving2^(n−1).')
low(1989,2,3.5,'Put the right angle at(0,0), other vertices(b,0),(0,c), with bc=2. The reflected points are(−b,0),(0,−c),(2bc²/(b²+c²),2b²c/(b²+c²)); the determinant gives area3.')
low(1989,3,3.5,'The printed first term is1989^1989. It has fewer than7956 digits, so a2<71604, a3≤45, and a4≤12. All terms are positive multiples of9, so a4=a5=9.')
a(1989,4,5,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Bijections'],r'Five ladders each have a monkey at the bottom and a banana at the top. Finitely many ropes join pairs of ladders, with no two ropes attached at the same point of a ladder. Each monkey climbs upward, taking every rope it meets to the other ladder and then resuming upward. Prove that every monkey reaches a different banana.',r'At each rope, pair the upward-arriving route on one ladder with the upward-departing route on the other, and vice versa. Thus every directed route segment has a unique successor and predecessor, except that bottom entrances have no predecessor and top exits have no successor. A route starting at a bottom cannot enter a cycle: the first repeated segment would already have a predecessor within the cycle and another from the entering route, contradicting uniqueness. Since there are finitely many segments, each route therefore ends at a top. Two bottom routes cannot merge, again by unique predecessors. The five routes thus reach the five distinct tops.','Ropes need not be horizontal, so height alone is not a valid monotone quantity. Reversible local routing rules exclude both cycles reached from an entrance and merging routes.')
a(1989,5,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Permutations & arrangements','Induction','Algebraic identities'] if False else ['Permutations & arrangements','Induction'],r'For each permutation $(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$ of $1,2,4,\ldots,2^{n-1}$, let $Q=\prod_{k=1}^n(X_1+\cdots+X_k)$. Find the sum of $1/Q$ over all permutations.',r'More generally, for positive numbers $a_1,\ldots,a_n$, the sum of reciprocal prefix products over labelled permutations is $1/(a_1\cdots a_n)$. Prove this by induction. Put $T=\sum a_i$ and group permutations according to their last entry $a_j$. Every final prefix contributes $T$, while induction gives $1/\prod_{i\ne j}a_i$ for the preceding prefixes. The total is $\frac1T\sum_j\frac1{\prod_{i\ne j}a_i}=1/\prod_i a_i$. For the given powers of2, the answer is $2^{-n(n-1)/2}$.','A stronger identity for arbitrary positive entries makes the sum collapse when permutations are grouped by their last element.')
a(1990,1,4,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Permutations & arrangements'],r'In each of $k$ rounds, $n\ge2$ players receive the distinct scores $1,2,\ldots,n$. Every player finishes with $26$ points. Determine all possible pairs $(n,k)$.',r'Total scores give $k(n+1)=52$, so the candidates are $(3,13),(12,4),(25,2),(51,1)$. The last is impossible because one round has distinct scores. For $(25,2)$, give reverse orders in the two rounds so every total is26. For $(12,4)$, use two reverse-order pairs, each contributing13 per player. For $(3,13)$, use three cyclic orders of $(1,2,3)$ so every player gains6, followed by five reverse-order pairs, each contributing4. Thus exactly the first three pairs work.','The total-score condition is necessary but not sufficient; constructions handle the odd-round case and exclude the single-round candidate.')
a(1990,2,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Probability','Counting'],r'Distinct numbers are placed uniformly at random in rows of lengths $1,2,\ldots,n$. Let $M_k$ be the maximum in row $k$. Find the probability that $M_1<M_2<\cdots<M_n$.',r'The greatest number must lie in the last row, an event of probability $n/[n(n+1)/2]=2/(n+1)$. Conditional on this event, the relative order of all entries in the first $n-1$ rows remains uniform, so the same argument applies recursively. Thus $p_n=2p_{n-1}/(n+1)$ with $p_1=1$, giving $p_n=2^n/(n+1)!$.','The row maxima are not independent. Conditioning on the position of the global maximum preserves the smaller uniform problem and yields the exact product.')
a(1990,3,4.5,'Geometry',['Circles & tangency','Trigonometry'],r'The diagonals of a cyclic convex quadrilateral $ABCD$ meet at $X$. Let $A^{\prime},B^{\prime},C^{\prime},D^{\prime}$ be the perpendicular projections of $X$ onto $AB,BC,CD,DA$, respectively. Prove $A^{\prime}B^{\prime}+C^{\prime}D^{\prime}=A^{\prime}D^{\prime}+B^{\prime}C^{\prime}$.',r'The points $X,A^{\prime},B^{\prime},B$ lie on the circle with diameter $XB$. Hence $A^{\prime}B^{\prime}=XB\sin B$. Likewise $C^{\prime}D^{\prime}=XD\sin D=XD\sin B$, because opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary. Their sum is $BD\sin B$. The other two lengths sum similarly to $AC\sin A$. If the circumradius is $R$, then $BD=2R\sin A$ and $AC=2R\sin B$, so the sums agree.','Each pair of adjacent projections lies on a diameter circle; converting the four pedal lengths to sine products lets cyclicity cancel the two sums.')
a(1990,4,4.5,'Geometry',['Constructions & loci','Geometric inequalities'],r'A particle starts at the origin. It may move at speed at most $2$ on the $x$-axis and speed at most $1$ elsewhere. Describe exactly the region it can reach in one second.',r'The region is the convex hull of the unit disk and the points $(2,0),(-2,0)$. Indeed, if the particle last leaves the axis at $(a,0)$, reaching that point costs at least $|a|/2$ seconds, so its endpoint lies in the disk centred there of radius $1-|a|/2$, where $|a|\le2$. Conversely every point in one of those disks is reached by travelling along the axis and then straight to it. For $a\ge0$, such a disk is $(a/2)(2,0)+(1-a/2)$ times the unit disk; similarly for $a\le0$, proving the convex-hull description. The upper boundary is $y=\sqrt{1-x^2}$ for $|x|\le1/2$, and $y=(2-|x|)/\sqrt3$ for $1/2\le|x|\le2$; reflect it in the axis for the lower boundary. The junction points are $(\pm1/2,\pm\sqrt3/2)$.','The fastest route may use the axis before leaving it; optimizing that departure point creates tangent line segments joined to a circular arc.')
hold(1990,5,'The recursively defined slow-growing sequence appears to have each positive value m repeated1+v_2(m) times, which would put value1025 uniquely at index2048. A complete proof of this run-length law and the requested increment assertions has not been verified. No numerical rating or publishable answer assigned.')
low(1991,1,3.5,'Use the seed3²+(−1)^5=2³ and weighted scaling: (x,y,z)=(3t^15,−t^6,2t^10) gives infinitely many nonzero integer solutions.')
low(1991,2,3.5,'The leading bit contributes2^(2n−1)·C(2n−1,n−1); every other position is1 in C(2n−2,n−2) strings, giving the remaining contribution(2^(2n−1)−1)·C(2n−2,n−2). For n=1 the answer is2.')
low(1991,3,3,'The midpoint of each chord is the perpendicular projection of the circle centre O onto its line through P, so it lies on the circle with diameter OP. If P=O all midpoints coincide at O, which is contained in a circle.')
a(1991,4,4,'Combinatorics',['Graph theory','Parity'],r'Take two disjoint copies of $K_{2,3}$. In each, call the two degree-three vertices top and bottom. Join the two tops and join the two bottoms, giving a graph with ten vertices and fourteen edges. Can its vertices be labelled by ten distinct numbers from $0,1,\ldots,14$ so that all absolute edge differences are distinct?',r'No. Each edge difference is a positive integer at most14, so distinctness would make the differences exactly $1,\ldots,14$, with odd sum105. But modulo2, an edge difference equals the sum of its endpoint labels. Summing over edges gives $\sum_v\deg(v)\,\ell(v)$, which is even because every degree in this graph is2 or4. This contradiction proves impossibility.','The diagram is an all-even-degree graph; summing edge differences modulo2 creates a global obstruction to the proposed distinct labels.')
a(1991,5,4.5,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Combinatorial geometry'],r'An equilateral triangle of side $n$ is subdivided by the standard triangular grid of unit side length. How many parallelograms have all their sides on grid lines?',r'There are three choices for the pair of grid directions used. For one choice, use lattice coordinates in which the triangle is $i,j\ge0$, $i+j\le n$. A parallelogram is determined by its lower corner $(i,j)$ and positive integer side lengths $a,b$, with $i+j+a+b\le n$. Writing $a^{\prime}=a-1$, $b^{\prime}=b-1$ and adding a slack variable gives five nonnegative integers summing to $n-2$, hence $\binom{n+2}{4}$ choices. Multiplying by3 gives $3\binom{n+2}{4}$, with value0 for $n=1$.','Counting by orientation avoids overlap, and a lattice-coordinate parametrization turns containment in the large triangle into a stars-and-bars constraint.')
a(1992,1,4,'Number Theory',['Divisibility & GCD','Primes','Factorials & binomial coefficients'],r'Prove that $1+2+\cdots+n$ divides $n!$ if and only if $n+1$ is not an odd prime.',r'The divisibility is equivalent to $n+1\mid2(n-1)!$. Put $m=n+1$. If $m$ is an odd prime, it cannot divide that product. If $m=2k$ is even, we need $k\mid(2k-2)!$, immediate for $k\ge2$ and true directly for $k=1$. If $m$ is odd composite, write $m=ab$ with $3\le a\le b$. If $a<b$, both factors occur in $(m-2)!$. If $a=b$, the distinct factors $a,2a$ both occur there because $2a\le a^2-2$ for $a\ge3$, so their product is divisible by $m$. This proves all cases.','Cancelling the triangular sum reduces the problem to a factorial divisibility criterion; odd prime squares need separate handling because a factor cannot be counted twice without justification.')
a(1992,2,4,'Algebra',['Inequalities','Algebraic identities'],r'For $x,y,z\ge0$, prove $x(x-z)^2+y(y-z)^2\ge(x-z)(y-z)(x+y-z)$, and determine equality.',r'After expansion the difference is the symmetric expression $S=x^3+y^3+z^3+3xyz-\sum_{sym}x^2y$. Order the variables as $x\ge y\ge z$. Then $S=(x-y)[x(x-z)-y(y-z)]+z(z-x)(z-y)$. Both terms are nonnegative. If $x>y$, the first is strictly positive. Thus equality requires $x=y$, and the second becomes $z(x-z)^2$, which vanishes exactly when $z=0$ or $x=z$. By symmetry, equality means all three variables equal, or one is zero and the other two are equal.','Expanding reveals a symmetric cubic inequality hidden by an asymmetric statement; ordering the variables gives a nonnegative factorization and all equality cases.')
a(1992,3,4.5,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Area & dissection','Geometric inequalities'],r'In square $ABCD$, choose interior points $U\in AB$ and $V\in CD$. Let $P=UD\cap AV$ and $Q=UC\cap BV$. Determine all choices maximizing the area of $PUQV$.',r'Normalize to $A=(0,1),B=(0,0),C=(1,0),D=(1,1)$, $U=(0,u)$, $V=(1,v)$ with $0<u,v<1$. Intersecting lines gives $P=((1-u)/(2-u-v),(1-uv)/(2-u-v))$ and $Q=(u/(u+v),uv/(u+v))$. The diagonal determinant formula for $PUQV$ simplifies to $[PUQV]=\frac14-\frac{(u-v)^2}{4(u+v)(2-u-v)}$. The denominator is positive, so the maximum is one quarter of the square’s area, attained exactly when $u=v$, or equivalently $AU=DV$ and $BU=CV$.','Two moving intersections make direct area comparisons awkward; coordinates reduce the deficit from the maximum to a square divided by a positive quantity.')
low(1992,4,3.5,'Set t=x+1 and u=t+1/t. The equation reduces to u²−2u−4=0; only u=1+√5 has |u|≥2. Thus x=(√5−1±√(2+2√5))/2.')
a(1992,5,5.5,'Combinatorics',['Permutations & arrangements','Parity','Algorithms & constructions'],r'Place two cards of each label $1,\ldots,n$, together with a joker, in a row of $2n+1$ positions with the joker in the middle. The two cards labelled $k$ must be $k$ positions apart. Determine all possible $n\le10$, and give a congruence obstruction for general $n$.',r'Number the positions $1,\ldots,2n+1$, excluding the middle $n+1$. Their sum is $2n(n+1)$, which is even. If the two positions for label $k$ are $a_k<b_k$, then $a_k+b_k\equiv b_k-a_k=k\pmod2$. Summing forces $n(n+1)/2$ even, hence $n\equiv0$ or3 modulo4. Thus among $1\le n\le10$ only $3,4,7,8$ can work. They all do, as these rows show, with J the joker: $n=3$: 2,3,2,J,3,1,1; $n=4$: 2,4,2,3,J,4,3,1,1; $n=7$: 5,7,2,6,2,5,4,J,7,6,4,3,1,1,3; $n=8$: 7,8,4,2,6,2,4,7,J,8,6,5,3,1,1,3,5. Each pair is separated by its own label. The parity argument proves impossibility for every $n\equiv1,2\pmod4$.','The middle vacancy changes the total-position parity; combining that obstruction with explicit verified arrangements resolves every requested size through10.')
a(1993,1,4.5,'Number Theory',['Diophantine equations','Squares & powers'],r'Find a triangle whose three side lengths and an altitude are four consecutive integers, where the altitude splits it into two integer-sided right triangles. Prove uniqueness.',r'Write the altitude as $h$. The two incident sides exceed $h$, so they lie among $h+1,h+2,h+3$, and the base is at least $h-1$. Each horizontal leg of the two right triangles is at most $\sqrt{(h+3)^2-h^2}=\sqrt{6h+9}$. Hence $h-1\le2\sqrt{6h+9}$, giving $h^2-26h-35\le0$ and therefore $h\le27$. Checking $h=1,\ldots,27$ and side $s=h+1,h+2,h+3$ for square $s^2-h^2$ gives exactly $(h,s,\text{other leg})=(3,5,4),(4,5,3),(8,10,6),(12,13,5),(12,15,9),(15,17,8),(24,25,7),(24,26,10)$. Two distinct incident sides at one height are possible only for $h=12$ or24. At12, the legs5 and9 sum to14, giving sides13,14,15. At24, the legs7 and10 sum to17, inconsistent with four consecutive lengths. Thus the unique triangle has sides13,14,15 and altitude12.','A geometric size bound makes the integer search finite and small; checking integer right-triangle legs leaves two heights, and the base condition singles out one.')
a(1993,2,4,'Algebra',['Rationality & irrationality','Sequences & recurrences'],r'Prove that a real number $x$ is rational exactly when three distinct terms of $x,x+1,x+2,\ldots$ form a geometric progression.',r'If $x+i,x+j,x+k$ form a geometric progression, then $(x+j)^2=(x+i)(x+k)$, so $(2j-i-k)x=ik-j^2$. The coefficient cannot vanish: if it did, $i+k=2j$ and $ik=j^2$ would force $i=j=k$. Thus $x$ is rational. Conversely write $x=a/b$ with integers $a$ and $b>0$. Choose $N\ge0$ so $r=x+N=c/b>0$. The three numbers $r,r(b+1),r(b+1)^2$ have successive offsets from $r$ equal to $0,c,c(b+2)$, all nonnegative integers. They are distinct terms of the original sequence and form a geometric progression.','The forward implication needs to rule out a vanishing coefficient; the converse constructs terms even when the original rational number is negative.')
a(1993,3,4,'Geometry',['Coordinates & vectors','Trigonometry','Geometric inequalities'],r'In triangle $ABC$, the medians to $AB$ and $AC$ are perpendicular. Prove $\cot B+\cot C\ge2/3$.',r'Put the centroid at the origin and the two perpendicular median lines on the coordinate axes. Then $B=(p,0)$, $C=(0,q)$, and $A=(-p,-q)$, with $pq\ne0$. Thus $BC^2=p^2+q^2$ and twice the area is $3|pq|$. In any triangle, $\cot B+\cot C=BC^2/(2[ABC])$, by projecting the altitude onto $BC$. Therefore $\cot B+\cot C=(p^2+q^2)/(3|pq|)\ge2/3$, with equality when $|p|=|q|$.','The centroid makes the perpendicular-median condition into coordinate axes; the cotangent sum becomes a simple squared-length ratio.')
a(1993,4,4,'Combinatorics',['Counting','Parity','Extremal arguments'],r'Players from different schools each play one tennis match; players from the same school do not meet. The total numbers of boys and girls differ by at most one, and the number of same-sex matches differs from the number of mixed matches by at most one. What is the greatest possible number of schools with an odd number of players?',r'For school $i$, put $d_i=b_i-g_i$, and let $D=\sum_i d_i$. Then $|D|\le1$. The difference between same-sex and mixed matches is $\sum_{i<j}d_i d_j=(D^2-\sum_i d_i^2)/2$, whose absolute value is at most1. Hence $\sum_i d_i^2\le D^2+2\le3$. Every odd-sized school has odd, nonzero $d_i$, contributing at least1, so there are at most three. Three schools containing one boy, one boy, and one girl attain the bound: there is one same-sex match and two mixed matches.','Encoding each school by its gender imbalance turns both global balance conditions into a sum-of-squares bound that counts odd-sized schools.')
a(1993,5,4,'Combinatorics',['Bijections','Recurrences & generating functions'],r'Define $y_1=1$. For $k\ge1$, set $(y_{2k},y_{2k+1})=(2y_k,2y_k+1)$ when $k$ is even, and $(2y_k+1,2y_k)$ when $k$ is odd. Prove that every positive integer occurs exactly once in the sequence.',r'Induct on binary length to show $y_n=n\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}\lfloor n/2\rfloor$, where xor adds each binary digit modulo2 without carrying. Appending a bit $b$ to the binary representation of $k$ appends to this xor value the bit $b$ xor the last bit of $k$, exactly as in the recurrence. This map is invertible: if the output bits from most significant to least are $c_m,\ldots,c_0$, recover the input bits by $b_m=c_m$ and $b_i=c_i$ xor $b_{i+1}$. The leading bit remains1, so the inverse gives one positive integer for every positive output.','The parity-dependent recursion is an adjacent-bit transform. An explicit inverse, rather than mere injectivity on initial blocks, proves every positive value occurs exactly once.')
ids={p['id'] for p in new};data=[p for p in data if p['id'] not in ids]+new
for y,rs in rr.items():
 for key in ['fullyScreened','partiallyScreened']:c[key]=[p for p in c[key] if not(p['competition']=='CMO' and p['year']==y)]
 entry=dict(competition='CMO',year=y,ratings=rs,source=f'https://cms.math.ca/wp-content/uploads/2019/07/exam{y}.pdf',assessmentNotes=nn[y])
